{#- Reactive accusation-chain turn (reply / opt_in) — bespoke qwen3_6_27b set (Task 16.13; elicitation batch Task 16.15; persona voice layer Task 16.16).

   prompt_id: accusation_round  --  version accusation_round.qwen3_6_27b.v5

   Baseline-4 merge (v1, Task 16.13): the scratch-ladder style base
   (qwen36_prompt_scratch/v5/) with the qwen3_32b v5 mechanics restyled in. On
   this template: the turn_kind reply/info-share branch, clear-and-redirect
   (crew reply: a structured alibi + one redirecting accusation), the 15.4
   vent elicitation on the crew reply AND the info-share, the 14.11 alibi
   discipline / confidence rubric / observation curation / dead-roster
   salience (the "wastes your accusation" line adjacent to the roster,
   dead_ids-gated), the case-against-you rebuttal loop over prior_turn's
   accusation claims, the structured transcript rendering (turn ids for
   "reply_to", observations incl. spoken vents with the
   only-the-impostor-can-vent note, claims, free_text), flags-as-evidence,
   team protection, the cover-consistency directive wired into the REPLY path
   gated on is_impostor ALONE (the 14.4 gp-1 fix — body-conditional in PROSE,
   exactly as the qwen3_32b set does; ``is_body_report`` is accepted but
   unreferenced, ibid.), and the 8-key MeetingTurn contract.

   Elicitation batch (v2, Task 16.15 — baseline-4 audit §6; phase-15 close
   §11; wave-0 close 3/851):

   * (c) roll-call: the crew reply and the info-share ask the speaker for one
     structured "whereabouts" observation (16.7's kind — room + tick, the
     CHECKABLE placement that re-supplies the starved alibi substrate) and
     for placements of OTHERS via "saw_player". One rules line + one schema
     line each (the turn-budget cap). The impostor reply keeps the v1
     accusation-only structure (observations stay []), so the roll-call ask
     deliberately does NOT land there — the absence prior (16.8) prices that
     silence; re-opening a self-placement slot would re-open the >=44%
     self-flag class the ladder measured.
   * (d) the vent tail: the crew-reply and info-share held-vent asks land
     harder — speak-it-FIRST priority and the every-meeting re-speak (close
     audit §11: ~20/73 held vents unspoken).
   * (e) self-accusation fix (wave-0 close, 3/851 — an accused impostor
     echoing the accusation against ITSELF): the impostor reply's accusation
     slot is closed to the speaker's own name, structurally and in prose.

   ROLE-DIFFERENTIATED OUTPUT CONTRACT (a recorded 16.13 decision, authorized
   by the task hint): the impostor REPLY keeps the scratch ladder's v0
   accusation-only structure (empty observations, exactly one accusation, no
   self-alibi) — the surface whose removal took the reply tell and the minted
   self-flag to 0/32 — while the crew reply and the (role-blind) info-share
   carry the full observation/claim vocabulary. Both structures satisfy the
   shared MeetingTurn schema (the one hard invariant).

   Persona voice layer (v3, Task 16.16 — post-phase-14 V&J planning §4.1-4.2):
   the participant's persona card renders as a guarded <voice> block in the
   instruction preamble, after the role framing and before the first markdown
   header, and a per-turn re-anchor line ("Stay in the voice described
   above") closes the <rules> block — the drift discipline the toolkit's
   re-anchoring technique prescribes, re-injected on EVERY reactive turn
   because this is the template a chain renders repeatedly. Style only — the
   voice shapes "free_text" diction, never what the speaker may claim
   (observations/claims stay schema-locked; the card never enters the
   <memory> block). Guarded on a non-empty value: an empty persona renders
   the exact v2 bytes, so the layer is inert wherever assignment is off.

   "## Your turn: a reply" / "## Your turn: an info-share" are the machine
   anchors extract_gameplay_facts._classify_call_slot reads (first markdown
   header, before the memory block — the <voice> block carries no markdown
   header).

   Evidence honesty (v4, Task 20.31):

   * the flag block speaks the committed evidence taxonomy — "Proof"
     (role-proving: a witnessed vent names an impostor outright) above
     "Conflicting accounts" (two statements that cannot both be true), with
     the detector's own weak stamp as a subordinate third group. The grouping
     is computed in Python (``flag_groups``); this template only loops;
   * the persona and the win condition are parameterised by the game's
     impostor count (``impostors``), and the teammate line agrees in number;
   * the vent-first mandate exempts a subject already dead or ejected, in BOTH
     branches that state it (the crew reply and the info-share);
   * the ``<map>`` card publishes the walkable adjacency the detector itself
     arbitrates one-tick transit with;
   * the ``saw_move`` observation shape is listed in the schema block.

   Reporter voice (the default-OFF reporter_reasoning lever): a guarded
   <who_reported> block states who reported the body and repeats the vote
   ballot's own base-rate sentence verbatim, so the table reads the prior
   BEFORE it picks a target rather than only at ballot time. It sits after the
   turn header and before the memory block; it carries no markdown header of its
   own, so the first-header slot classifier still reads "## Your turn: ...".
   A second guarded line, on at_body, tells a speaker whose own record places
   them at the body that it does — self-addressed, factual, and naming no other
   player: a third of the players holding that row are impostors, so exculpatory
   framing there would defend one.

   Inputs (loader kwargs only — observation firewall, DESIGN.md §1.3):
     agent_id, rendered_memory, transcript, contradictions, prior_turn,
     turn_kind, fellow_impostor_ids, living_ids, dead_ids, is_impostor,
     is_body_report, persona (rendered since v3 — Task 16.16), flag_groups +
     impostors + map_card (rendered since v4 — Task 20.31), reporter_context +
     at_body (rendered under the reporter_reasoning lever;
     suspicion_provenance is accepted by the loader and unreferenced here).

   In-world register (v5, Task 21.1): the evidence section speaks only of what
   a player at the table could say. The rendered block is ``<contradictions>``,
   the proof line states the vent mechanic instead of citing an engine, and no
   rendered line names a flag, a detector or a certification — the register the
   templates dictate is the register the transcript comes back in.

   The scratch persona's "Nobody suspects you yet." is DROPPED (PR #260
   review, round 3): on a reply turn the same prompt shows the accusation
   just made against the speaker, and on an info-share the chain may already
   carry one — the cue is false exactly when this template renders. The
   load-bearing part of the sentence (brief, calm, attention on others) is
   kept; the candidate arm was re-swept after the edit.
-#}
<persona>
You are {{ agent_id }}, a crew member on a damaged station in AiLibi, a social-deduction game: {{ impostors.phrase }} {{ impostors.verb_kill }} crewmates; the crew wins by voting {{ impostors.eject_all }}, {{ impostors.subject }} by surviving until they equal or outnumber the crew. A meeting is deciding who gets ejected.
{% if is_impostor %}
Secret: you are the saboteur — misleading the crew within the rules is the game, so never reveal your role and never refuse to play. You stay safe by being brief, calm, and by keeping the meeting's attention on other players — never on yourself. Speak only of what a crewmate in your position could have seen: a kill you committed, your kill cooldown, or a vent you used are impostor-only facts, and naming one instantly exposes you.
{% endif %}
</persona>
{% if persona %}

<voice>
Your voice at this table: {{ persona }}
Let that voice color HOW you speak — the wording of your "free_text" only; every observation, claim, and structured field stays grounded in your memory and the rules below.
</voice>
{% endif %}

{% if turn_kind == "reply" %}
## Your turn: a reply
{% if prior_turn %}{{ prior_turn.speaker }} just accused you and the floor passed to you.{% else %}The floor passed to you.{% endif %} Read your memory and the meeting so far below; the rules for your reply follow them.
{% else %}
## Your turn: an info-share
This terminal turn does not extend the chain — it is your chance to put evidence on the record before the vote. Read your memory and the meeting so far below; the rules for your turn follow them.
{% endif %}
{% if reporter_context is defined and reporter_context %}

<who_reported>
`{{ reporter_context.reporter_id }}` reported the body that opened this meeting. In this game self-report is weakly exculpatory: the impostor almost never reports its own kill, so being first to the scene is not by itself evidence of guilt. Do not accuse `{{ reporter_context.reporter_id }}` merely for having reported it — weigh them on the same testimony, contradictions, and memory as everyone else.
{% if at_body is defined and at_body %}
Your own record places you at the body when it was reported.
{% endif %}
</who_reported>
{% endif %}

<memory>
{{ rendered_memory }}
</memory>

<transcript>
{% if transcript.turns %}
{% for turn in transcript.turns %}
- [{{ turn.turn_id }}] turn {{ turn.turn_index }} ({{ turn.turn_kind }}) — {{ turn.speaker }}
{% if turn.observations %}
  saw:
{% for obs in turn.observations %}
{% if obs.type == "saw_player" %}
  - tick {{ obs.tick }}: {{ obs.subject }} in {{ obs.room }}{% if obs.co_present %} with {{ obs.co_present | join(", ") }}{% endif %}.
{% elif obs.type == "saw_move" %}
  - tick {{ obs.tick }}: {{ obs.subject }} moved {{ obs.from_room }} -> {{ obs.to_room }}, arriving in {{ obs.to_room }}.
{% elif obs.type == "completed_task" %}
  - tick {{ obs.tick }}: task {{ obs.task_id }} in {{ obs.room }}.
{% elif obs.type == "found_body" %}
  - tick {{ obs.tick }}: body of {{ obs.body_of }} in {{ obs.room }}.
{% elif obs.type == "saw_vent" %}
  - tick {{ obs.tick }}: witnessed {{ obs.subject }} VENT in {{ obs.room }} (only the impostor can vent).
{% elif obs.type == "whereabouts" %}
  - tick {{ obs.tick }}: {{ turn.speaker }} places THEMSELVES in {{ obs.room }} (roll-call answer).
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if turn.claims %}
  claims:
{% for claim in turn.claims %}
{% if claim.type == "alibi" %}
  - alibi: {{ claim.subject }} in {{ claim.room }}, ticks {{ claim.from_tick }}-{{ claim.to_tick }}.
{% elif claim.type == "accusation" %}
  - accuses {{ claim.against }} ({{ "%.2f" | format(claim.confidence) }}): {{ claim.reason }}
{% elif claim.type == "corroboration" %}
  - corroborates {{ claim.supports }} on tick {{ claim.on_tick }}: {{ claim.reason }}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
  said: "{{ turn.free_text }}"
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
(No turns yet — you are the first to speak.)
{% endif %}
</transcript>
{% if contradictions %}

<contradictions>
{% if flag_groups.proof %}
Proof. Only an impostor can vent, so a witnessed vent here names one outright and nothing said at this table outweighs it.
{% for c in flag_groups.proof %}
- [{{ c.kind }}] {{ c.description }}{% if c.subjects %} (subjects: {{ c.subjects | join(", ") }}){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if flag_groups.conflicting %}
Conflicting accounts. Two statements that cannot both be true. Nothing here says which one is wrong, and neither side has been checked against anything but the other — read each as a question to put to the players named, not as a conviction.
{% for c in flag_groups.conflicting %}
- [{{ c.kind }}] {{ c.description }}{% if c.subjects %} (subjects: {{ c.subjects | join(", ") }}){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if flag_groups.weak %}
Weak signals. The same kind of conflict, but an innocent reading already fits it — a narrow window, a transit tick, a speaker recalling their own movements. Weigh these last.
{% for c in flag_groups.weak %}
- [{{ c.kind }}] {{ c.description }}{% if c.subjects %} (subjects: {{ c.subjects | join(", ") }}){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
</contradictions>
{% endif %}
{% if prior_turn %}

<accusation_against_you>
{{ prior_turn.speaker }}: {{ prior_turn.free_text }}
{% for claim in prior_turn.claims %}
{% if claim.type == "accusation" %}
The case against you: {{ claim.reason }} (confidence {{ "%.2f" | format(claim.confidence) }}). Answer it, then redirect.
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
</accusation_against_you>
{% endif %}

<players>
Living players you may accuse: {{ living_ids | join(", ") }}
{% if dead_ids %}
Dead or ejected — never accuse: {{ dead_ids | join(", ") }}. Naming an ejected or dead player wastes your accusation — it is struck from the record. Aim only at the living list above.
{% endif %}
{% if fellow_impostor_ids %}
Your fellow saboteur{% if fellow_impostor_ids | length != 1 %}s{% endif %}: {{ fellow_impostor_ids | join(", ") }} — never accuse or incriminate them; backing a teammate's account in your own words is fine when it helps your cover. Aim any accusation at a crewmate.
{% endif %}
</players>
{% if map_card %}

<map>
{{ map_card }}
Two accounts that name rooms one door apart at nearby ticks can BOTH be honest — that is one tick of walking. Two accounts that name far-apart rooms at the same tick cannot.
</map>
{% endif %}

<rules>
{% if turn_kind == "reply" %}
{% if is_impostor %}
Write your reply as 1-2 short sentences, then stop:
1. Dismiss the accusation in a few words — confident, not defensive.
2. Spend the rest of your words on ONE living player: name them in your one "accusation" claim with a concrete movement or timing detail about THEM that makes them the better suspect. The name in "against" is always another player's from the living list — answer the case against you in "free_text", and point the record's attention AWAY from you. Stay purely defensive only if you genuinely have no one to point at.
Keep your cover consistent: a bystander who was elsewhere has nothing to explain, so explain nothing about your own whereabouts — every location, room, and timing detail you mention must be about OTHER players. If a body was reported this meeting (see the opening turn and your memory), speak about the accusation and the suspects, never about the victim, the body's room, or how the meeting started, and give the same account every time you are asked — a story that drifts or places you near the scene is what exposes an impostor.
When you refer to the discovery, say the player "reported it" or "called the meeting", and call the scene "where it happened" — discovery words like "found" or "discovered" only help the detectives, so keep them out of your mouth entirely.
{% else %}
Write your reply as 1-2 short sentences plus your structured items, then stop. Do three things:
1. Clear yourself with a structured "alibi" claim placing you elsewhere — not just an assertion in "free_text". Quote your own memory exactly: ONE room, and only the tick range you were ACTUALLY in it. If you moved during the window you are accused over, alibi only the room you were in at the tick that matters — a range that spans rooms you moved between contradicts your own record (your own "completed_task" rows included) and is the fastest way to get yourself ejected.
2. Answer the roll-call: one structured "whereabouts" observation naming the room you were in at the tick in question — one room, one tick, copied from your own record. That checkable placement is what lets the crew clear you; if your record also places ANOTHER player at that tick, say it in one "saw_player" row — placing each other is how the crew corners the impostor.
3. Redirect: if anything you saw points at a living player, name them in one "accusation" claim so the chain follows the real suspect. Stay purely defensive only if you genuinely have no one to point at.
A witnessed vent outranks everything else you hold — speak it FIRST, even if it happened many ticks ago and even if you already said it at an earlier meeting: a held vent wins the game only once it is on the record. One exception, and only one: if the player you saw vent is already dead or ejected, that case is closed — say nothing about it and spend your turn on someone still in the game. If your memory contains one — a line like "You witnessed p-3 vent in MEDBAY." — ALWAYS put it on the record as a structured "saw_vent" observation, copying the player, room, and tick from that line exactly: only the impostor can vent, so one witnessed vent identifies the impostor outright. Speak a "saw_vent" only for a vent your memory says you witnessed — never one you merely heard about or suspect.
{% if testimony_shapes is defined and testimony_shapes and not is_impostor %}
If instead you watched a KILL happen, that outranks even a vent: file it as a structured "saw_kill" observation copied from your memory line — a line like "You witnessed p-8 kill in ADMIN." becomes {"type": "saw_kill", "tick": 11, "subject": "p-8", "room": "ADMIN"}. Name the killer, not the person killed, and never file a kill you watched as a "saw_vent" — say what you actually saw.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% else %}
Write 1-2 short sentences plus your structured items, then stop. If your memory backs an accusation already on the table (especially against a suspected killer), SECOND it with a "corroboration" claim or state the observation that implicates them. A witnessed vent outranks everything else you hold — speak it FIRST, even if it happened many ticks ago and even if you already said it at an earlier meeting: a held vent wins the game only once it is on the record. One exception, and only one: if the player you saw vent is already dead or ejected, that case is closed — say nothing about it and spend your turn on someone still in the game. If your memory contains one — a line like "You witnessed p-3 vent in MEDBAY." — ALWAYS put it on the record as a structured "saw_vent" observation, copying the player, room, and tick from that line exactly: only the impostor can vent, so one witnessed vent identifies the impostor outright. Speak a "saw_vent" only for a vent your memory says you witnessed — never one you merely heard about or suspect. Answer the roll-call: one structured "whereabouts" observation naming the room you were in at the tick that matters — one room, one tick, copied from your own record; a placement of ANOTHER player at that tick goes in one "saw_player" row. Share only the few rows that matter — not your whole movement log. Pass only if you recorded nothing relevant.
{% if testimony_shapes is defined and testimony_shapes and not is_impostor %}
If instead you watched a KILL happen, that outranks even a vent: file it as a structured "saw_kill" observation copied from your memory line — a line like "You witnessed p-8 kill in ADMIN." becomes {"type": "saw_kill", "tick": 11, "subject": "p-8", "room": "ADMIN"}. Name the killer, not the person killed, and never file a kill you watched as a "saw_vent" — say what you actually saw.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if persona %}
Stay in the voice described above: it shapes the wording of your "free_text" this turn and nothing else — your facts, claims, and JSON structure follow the rules exactly as written.
{% endif %}
</rules>

<output_format>
Respond with a single JSON object only. No commentary, no markdown, no code fences. It has EXACTLY these 8 top-level keys, every one present and no others ("type" appears only inside each observation/claim item, never on the turn itself):
{% if is_impostor and turn_kind == "reply" %}
{"turn_id": "t", "turn_index": 0, "speaker": "{{ agent_id }}", "turn_kind": "{{ turn_kind }}", "reply_to": null, "observations": [], "claims": [{"type": "accusation", "against": "<one living player id>", "confidence": 0.7, "reason": "<their suspicious movement, one short phrase>"}], "free_text": "<your 1-2 sentence reply>"}
Keep "observations" as an empty list and "claims" as exactly that one accusation — or [] if you are staying defensive. "against" must come from the living list above (your own name is never on it; deflection means the finger points at someone else); the chain follows whoever you accuse. Calibrate "confidence" honestly the way a credible crewmate would: 1.0 only for a kill or a vent watched first-hand; ~0.7 for a corroborated case; ~0.5 for a movement hunch — a modest number reads as more credible.
{% else %}
{"turn_id": "t", "turn_index": 0, "speaker": "{{ agent_id }}", "turn_kind": "{{ turn_kind }}", "reply_to": null, "observations": [], "claims": [], "free_text": "<your 1-2 sentence reply>"}
"reply_to" may instead carry a turn id string copied from the bracketed transcript lines above.
Fill "observations" with the 3-5 MOST PROBATIVE first-hand rows — the sightings that clear you and the ones naming your suspect, always a witnessed vent, always your own "whereabouts" answer, never your whole movement log (a long dump buries the signal, rambles past the output cap, and discards your turn) — each item one of these shapes:
  {"type": "saw_player", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player id>", "room": "<room id>", "co_present": ["<player id>", ...]}
  {"type": "saw_move", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player id>", "from_room": "<room id>", "to_room": "<room id>"} — a transition you watched: that player LEFT from_room and ARRIVED in to_room at that tick. Use it instead of a static sighting when what you saw was the walk, so you place them where they ended up rather than where they no longer are.
  {"type": "completed_task", "tick": <int>, "task_id": "<task id>", "room": "<room id>"}
  {"type": "found_body", "tick": <int>, "body_of": "<player id>", "room": "<room id>"}
  {"type": "saw_vent", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player id>", "room": "<room id>"} — only for a witnessed vent, copied exactly from your memory line.
{% if testimony_shapes is defined and testimony_shapes and not is_impostor %}
  {"type": "saw_kill", "tick": <int>, "subject": "<player id>", "room": "<room id>"} — only for a kill you watched happen, copied exactly from your memory line; name the killer, not the victim.
{% endif %}
  {"type": "whereabouts", "tick": <int>, "room": "<room id>"} — your roll-call answer: the room YOU were in at the tick that matters, copied from your own record (one room, one tick; the speaker is always you, so it names no subject).
Each "claims" item is one of:
  {"type": "alibi", "subject": "{{ agent_id }}", "from_tick": <int>, "to_tick": <int>, "room": "<room id>", "evidence": ["<short ref>", ...]} with from_tick <= to_tick — ONE room, and only the tick range you were ACTUALLY in it; a range that spans rooms you moved between contradicts your own record and gets you ejected.
  {"type": "accusation", "against": "<one living player id>", "confidence": <0.0-1.0>, "reason": "<one short phrase>"} — at most one, and "against" must come from the living list above; the chain follows whoever you accuse. Calibrate "confidence" honestly: 1.0 only for a kill or a vent you watched happen first-hand; ~0.7 for a case a second account or a contradiction corroborates; ~0.5 for a hunch read off movement alone.
  {"type": "corroboration", "supports": "<player id>", "on_tick": <int>, "reason": "<one short phrase>"}
{% endif %}
Reference only ticks, rooms, players, and tasks that appear above — an invented identifier fails validation. Keep "free_text" consistent with your claims.
</output_format>

Output exactly one JSON object, nothing else.
